.TH "SKARN-GUARD" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.36.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-guard \- Pre\-execution hook: read an event on stdin, emit a verdict
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBguard\fR [report|accept <fingerprint>] [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Real\-time pre\-execution hook for AI coding assistants; reads an event on
stdin and emits a verdict. guard report summarizes the audit log and forecasts
what enforcing would cost. It recommends switching the hooks to enforce only
once the flagged calls in the window span five or more days with no deny or
would\-be deny recorded among them. A recorded ask does not stop the
recommendation, even on a host that answers an ask with a block in enforce
mode, and neither does a detect\-only record; the recommendation carries the
skarn setup command for each host setup wires and the manual step for any
other host. The guard logs nothing but flagged calls, so a window with none is
no evidence that the hook ran and gets no recommendation. At a terminal it
also names, once per release, a hook in your own per\-user assistant config
that has fallen behind this build. guard accept records a flagged finding as a
false positive so the guard stops flagging it.
.PP
Control the real\-time pre\-execution hook used for editor/agent integration
with Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, and
the Antigravity CLI. report reads the guard's own audit log rather than an
event. It summarizes the flagged calls per assistant (verdicts, top rules,
latency, the most recent denies), states what enforcing that window would have
cost in blocks per day and prompts per day, and prints the enforce flip once
the window spans at least five days with zero denies; for a host wired by
skarn setup, that flip is a skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce command,
which leaves plugin\-installed hooks in audit. Run at a terminal it reads one
thing more, your own per\-user hook configs, so it can name a hook that has
fallen behind this build once per release; piped or as json it reads nothing
but the log. The guard logs only flagged calls, so every count and every rate
report prints is over flagged actions, never over all traffic. accept closes
that loop. An audit record whose top finding carries a secret gets a
fingerprint, the same secret\-scoped key check writes to SARIF
partialFingerprints; report prints it under each such deny, and `skarn guard
accept <fingerprint>` records it as a false positive so that finding stops
being flagged on the identical call. A secret\-less deny \- a structural
egress detector, a typosquat, a chain \- carries no fingerprint and no accept
line. Only the highest\-severity finding of a call carries the fingerprint, so
a call hiding several distinct secrets peels one accept at a time. The
structural egress detectors are built into the engine rather than the rule
pack, and they tier themselves. An egress whose whole payload is visible in
the command itself is held at the ask tier: a bare request, a literal body, or
query parameters, with no file sent, no credential store named, no earlier
secret read in the same call, and no payload bytes the command text does not
show. A command substitution, an environment expansion, a shell redirect, a
form read, or a pipe feeding the target all keep the deny, and so does a
payload carrying a recognized credential file or upload form, a co\-detected
secret, or recon earlier in the same call, with one exception. A dynamic
payload is reported at high rather than critical when its command names at
least one URL, every destination, proxy and connection override it names (\-x,
\-\-proxy, \-\-socks*, \-\-connect\-to, \-\-resolve, \-\-dns\-*,
\-\-interface, a *_proxy variable on the command line) is a loopback literal
(localhost, 127.x.x.x in canonical octets, ::1, 0.0.0.0), every command
substitution in it is a plain local read (printenv, env, cat and the like with
no pipe or nested command), and every stage of the pipeline is a known network
client or a known local filter with no config variable, unix socket, URL file
or unknown command; it is reported at high because a proxy variable inherited
from the environment, an HTTP redirect (\-L is not read as a reason to keep
critical) or a local forwarder can still carry the payload off the machine
with nothing in the command to show it. That finding never disappears, enforce
mode asks on it as on any other high finding, and a formed chain still denies.
Ask means a real approval prompt on the primary shell surfaces (Claude Code
PreToolUse, Copilot preToolUse on both the CLI and VS Code, Cursor shell and
MCP hooks) and a block on the channels with no ask (Codex, Claude Code
PermissionRequest, Copilot permissionRequest, Cursor file\-read events,
Gemini, Grok Build). Both subverbs are ungated: report reads the log, plus
your own hook configs at a terminal, and accept writes only a file. On a
flagged call the guard consults the personal accepted\-findings baseline it
shares with check (~/.config/skarn/baseline.json, the file form only, never
the org\-union directory, so the guard can never hit a paid gate). A finding
accepted as a false positive is dropped from the verdict. A finding a human
confirmed as a REAL leak (`skarn baseline audit`, label true\-positive) is
never suppressed and keeps being blocked until the credential is rotated. A
correlated attack chain still denies even when every secret in the call has
been accepted, and a baseline that is missing, unreadable, or malformed
suppresses nothing at all: a broken baseline means more blocking, never less.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-guard\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
audit reports only; enforce emits a deny or ask verdict. Enforce needs a
paid\-tier license; an unlicensed guard, or one on the free tier, runs
audit\-only. One of: audit, enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Gate Read operations (credential\-file reconnaissance) on the hosts that do
not gate them by default; Claude Code gates Read without this flag and scans
the file a Read names before the read runs. On every host, turn the ask the
guard gives an event it cannot fully classify (unparseable, oversized, a
repeated key, or a tool input nested past the flatten bound) into a deny.
Without it that event asks in enforce on every host, degraded to the host's
deny where it has no ask channel; a scan\-infrastructure failure (unreadable
rules) is unchanged by this flag on Claude, which asks, and fails closed only
with it on every other host.
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Hook host. By default, auto\-detect from the event shape. Pass codex for Codex
CLI and copilot for Copilot CLI: both send Claude\-shaped events that skarn
cannot tell apart from Claude's. Gemini CLI, Grok Build, the Antigravity CLI,
Kimi Code and Cline each send a payload key no other host sends, so they
auto\-detect too, and the shipped configs pin them anyway. On Kimi Code that
pin is load\-bearing rather than uniform policy: a host embedding kimi's older
hook engine sends no client_type, and its Claude\-shaped prompt event then
fails open under the Claude adapter. On Cline the shipped executables pin
\-\-agent cline all the same, because a hook file copied between machines and
forks carries nothing else that names the host. Grok Build is the one host
that overrides an explicit pin: an event delivered by grok's own hook runner
is scanned by the grok adapter even where the invoking config pins another
host, because grok runs Claude\-config and Cursor\-config hooks with its own
payload dialect. With \-\-self\-test, all (the default) tests the claude,
cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, antigravity, kimi, and cline adapters,
copilot on both its CLI and VS Code dialects. With report, this option
restricts the summary to one assistant, repeatable or comma\-separated, and
copilot\-code selects the records the copilot adapter labels when a Copilot in
Code hook runs with a COPILOT_HOME that names a Code profile. One of: claude,
cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, antigravity, kimi, cline, all.
.TP
\fB\-\-event\fR \fIname\fR
The hook event this config entry fires on, for a host whose payload does not
name its own event. The Copilot CLI needs it: its permissionRequest payload
carries a subset of preToolUse's documented fields and nothing separates the
two, so a permissionRequest entry must say so or the guard reads it as a
preToolUse and answers in a schema that event does not understand. Default:
the event is inferred from the payload shape, which never yields
permissionRequest. Codex names its own event and the pin never overrides that
name; skarn reads the pin only where the name is missing, so the deny for a
payload that never parsed, or a call carrying no hook_event_name, takes the
pinned shape instead of the PreToolUse default, with or without \-\-strict.
Claude honors the pin the same way for a payload that never parsed: a
permissionRequest pin answers with the PermissionRequest behavior deny, and
with no pin the PreToolUse ask stands, which another event may ignore. Ignored
by every other host, and by the VS Code Copilot dialect. One of: preToolUse,
permissionRequest.
.TP
\fB\-\-self\-test\fR
Verify the guard's own code path instead of reading an event. Skarn feeds
synthetic fixture events through each adapter's real code path, then reports
the verdicts, latency, and a guard\-log write probe. Every adapter gets three:
one benign, one carrying a fake credential, and one shell call that echoes the
base64 encoding of a fake AWS secret key, which passes only when the
aws\-secret\-access\-key rule denies it from the decoded payload. Every
adapter also gets a deep fixture, a tool input nested past the guard's flatten
bound, which passes only when the adapter marks it unclassifiable and gives it
the verdict every unclassifiable call gets (ask, deny under \-\-strict),
printed on the line as deep nesting ask, deep nesting deny, or deep nesting
would\-ask in audit. Every adapter whose host hands the hook the tool output
gets a fourth, a post\-tool event whose output carries a fake GCP
service\-account key and a fake AWS secret key, which passes only when the
gcp\-service\-account\-key rule denies it from the tool result and the
aws\-secret\-access\-key rule denies the AWS key as found in the tool result
itself: claude, codex, copilot on both dialects, gemini, grok, and cline. The
cursor output fixture is a beforeReadFile event, which the guard gates only
under \-\-strict, so it runs only with \-\-strict. Kimi and antigravity carry
no output fixture: the kimi adapter gates no post\-tool event, and an
antigravity PostToolUse carries no tool output. Each expected rule must deny
on its own and from the named source, so a deny produced by another rule, a
dead input decode, an adapter that drops the tool output, or a dead
tool\-result scan fails the line. The kimi fixtures carry kimi's own
client_type stamp and content\-block prompt shape, so they prove the routing
the shipped pin also asserts. The mode in the header is this invocation's,
from \-\-guard\-mode and the license; it is never read from a host config, so
a bare run reports audit whatever skarn setup wired, and skarn doctor is where
the wired mode is reported. The fake credential is only ever printed redacted,
and the self\-test deliberately ignores the accepted\-findings baseline, so an
accepted fingerprint can never make a dead hook look alive. What it proves is
the binary, the adapter and the log write. It runs entirely in this process
and reads no host config, so it never proves that a host is calling the hook:
skarn doctor reads the host configs for that, and on a host doctor does not
cover (kimi today) the only proof is a flagged action driven through the host
itself. Exit 0 when every adapter passes, 1 otherwise; works unlicensed, with
audit semantics.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
On the hook invocation, `skarn guard \-\-log <path|none>` names where this
call appends its redacted JSONL record. A path takes precedence over
$SKARN_GUARD_LOG; none writes no record even when that variable is set; no
flag at all falls back to the variable. Skarn refuses a path that expands to a
parent\-directory segment or carries a control character: no record is written
and there is no fallback destination, because a fallback would derive from
$XDG_CACHE_HOME or $HOME, which the same hook command can also set. With
report: a guard log to summarize, repeatable. Default: $SKARN_GUARD_LOG when
set, otherwise the log skarn setup wires for each assistant
($XDG_CACHE_HOME/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl, plus the home cache beside
it when a log already exists there). A log that does not exist is named in the
output with the command that wires it, never silently counted as zero, and a
configured path the guard refuses is named as refused rather than shown as
empty.
.TP
\fB\-\-window\fR \fIspan\fR
With report: how far back to summarize, as <N>d or all. The default is 7d.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With report: text renders the summary, the enforce\-cost forecast, and the
recommendation. json emits the whole aggregate as one object with stable field
names for fleet scripts, including a forecast object and the denominator
string that names what its rates are rates of. One of: text, json. The default
is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIfile\fR
With accept: the accepted\-findings file to write. Defaults to the personal
baseline the guard itself reads (~/.config/skarn/baseline.json, honoring
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME), created on first use. `skarn baseline accept` always names
its file instead, because an accept the guard does not read would not stop the
block. Always a file, never a directory: the org\-union form is not reachable
from the guard. Accepting a fingerprint already recorded as a confirmed true
positive is refused, so a fingerprint\-only accept can never re\-silence a
real leak.
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
With accept: why this finding is a false positive, recorded in the baseline
entry. Do not paste a secret here; the text is stored verbatim.
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
With accept: override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the entry; defaults
to $SKARN_IDENTITY.
.SH "HOOK EVENTS"
Every hook event skarn guard handles, one table per host. Fires on is what
triggers the event. Enforce is what enforce mode does with a finding: deny or
ask, deny (a would\-be ask becomes a deny), cancel task (the whole task ends),
advises only, or log only (the host gives the hook no way to block, so only
the guard log records the verdict). Shipped says whether the integration
configs wire the event or leave it opt\-in. Audit mode never blocks on a
finding; the line above each table says how it speaks and how a guard failure
lands.
.SS "Claude Code"
Audit mode advises without deciding. A guard crash or timeout fails open on
Claude Code unless the hook entry sets onFailure to block (on Windows without
Git Bash a failed shell\-form PreToolUse or PermissionRequest command hook
blocks), and an event the guard cannot classify asks in enforce (denies under
\-\-strict).
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&PermissionRequest	\&permission check	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptExpansion	\&expanded command	\&deny	\&yes
\&TaskCreated	\&new task	\&deny	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
\&PostToolBatch	\&tool result batch	\&deny	\&yes
\&Stop	\&response end	\&advises only	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Cursor"
Audit mode is silent. beforeReadFile is gated only under \-\-strict, which the
shipped config passes on that event alone. In enforce an unparseable event
exits 2 and an input the guard cannot fully classify asks (denies under
\-\-strict).
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&beforeShellExecution	\&shell command	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&beforeReadFile	\&file read	\&deny	\&yes
\&beforeMCPExecution	\&MCP tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&beforeSubmitPrompt	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Codex CLI"
Codex shares Claude's event names, so pass \-\-agent codex. Audit mode advises
without deciding. In enforce an event the guard cannot classify is denied in
that event's own schema.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&PermissionRequest	\&permission check	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
\&Stop	\&turn end	\&advises only	\&yes
.TE
.SS "GitHub Copilot CLI"
Audit mode advises on preToolUse and postToolUse and is silent on the rest. A
hook loading after Skarn can replace a permissionRequest deny with allow, so
preToolUse is the durable gate. preToolUse fails closed on a non\-zero exit
and open on a timeout. In enforce an unparseable event exits 2 and an input
the guard cannot fully classify asks.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&preToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&permissionRequest	\&permission check	\&deny	\&yes
\&userPromptSubmitted	\&submitted prompt	\&log only	\&yes
\&userPromptTransformed	\&transformed prompt	\&log only	\&opt\-in
\&preMcpToolCall	\&MCP tool call	\&log only	\&opt\-in
\&postToolUse	\&tool result	\&advises only	\&opt\-in
.TE
.SS "GitHub Copilot (VS Code agent mode)"
VS Code reads hooks from ~/.claude/settings.json, so a machine running the
Claude guard receives VS Code events too. Audit mode advises without deciding.
A non\-zero exit other than 2 fails open. In enforce an unparseable event
exits 2 and an input the guard cannot fully classify asks.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
.TE
.SS "Gemini CLI"
Audit mode advises without deciding. Entry timeouts are in milliseconds. Exit
2 blocks; any other non\-zero exit, and a hook that runs past its timeout,
fails open. In enforce an event the guard cannot classify is denied.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&BeforeTool	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&BeforeAgent	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&AfterTool	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
.TE
.SS "Grok Build"
Audit mode is silent. The snake_case rows are how grok's stdin spells the
three PascalCase events, and they fire wherever those are wired. Grok also
runs the hooks in the Claude and Cursor configs, and the guard routes those
events to grok whatever \-\-agent says. In enforce an unparseable event exits
2 and an input the guard cannot fully classify is denied.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&log only	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&log only	\&opt\-in
\&pre_tool_use	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&user_prompt_submit	\&submitted prompt	\&log only	\&yes
\&post_tool_use	\&tool result	\&log only	\&opt\-in
.TE
.SS "Antigravity CLI (agy)"
Audit mode answers allow with the would\-be verdict in the reason. A crashed,
slow or missing skarn blocks every matched call in either mode, and in enforce
an event the guard cannot classify is denied.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Kimi Code"
Audit mode is silent. Pass \-\-agent kimi, as the shipped entries do: a host
on kimi's older hook engine sends a payload shaped like Claude's. Only exit 2,
or exit 0 with a deny object, blocks; every other failure allows the action.
In enforce an unparseable event exits 2 and an input the guard cannot fully
classify is denied.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Cline"
Audit mode advises without deciding. The SDK runner behind the cline CLI and
4.x extension sessions sends the snake_case names for the same files and never
reads a prompt_submit answer, so a prompt blocks only under the extension's
own runner. In enforce an event the guard cannot classify is cancelled. The
shipped files are POSIX shell, so Windows is not covered.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&cancel task	\&yes
\&tool_call	\&tool call	\&cancel task	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&cancel task	\&yes
\&prompt_submit	\&submitted prompt	\&log only	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&cancel task	\&opt\-in
\&tool_result	\&tool result	\&cancel task	\&opt\-in
.TE
.SS "Copilot in Code"
Copilot in Code runs the Copilot CLI's hooks from each profile's hooks folder
and a folder project's .github/hooks. A preToolUse deny is honoured, a nonzero
exit fails closed, and a timeout let the held tool run. A would\-be ask is
answered with a deny on Code, where ask is unmeasured.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&preToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&userPromptSubmitted	\&submitted prompt	\&log only	\&yes
\&postToolUse	\&tool result	\&advises only	\&opt\-in
.TE
